# SETUP.md: new machine, read this first

You are an agent (or a person) on a machine that should become identical to every other machine of this user:
same SSH keys and ssh config (every box), same service credentials, same Claude Code skills, global CLAUDE.md,
settings and memory, same ontology. One script does all of it. Follow these steps in order and report what each printed.

Rule 1 of this user applies here too: spend the least money possible (it is in the restored ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md).

## 1. AWS CLI + login (the only human step)

Linux/WSL, no sudo needed:

    curl -fsSLo /tmp/awscli.zip "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-$(uname -m).zip"
    python3 -c "import zipfile,os;z=zipfile.ZipFile('/tmp/awscli.zip');[os.chmod(z.extract(i,'/tmp'),i.external_attr>>16&0o7777) for i in z.infolist()]"
    /tmp/aws/install -i ~/.local/aws-cli -b ~/.local/bin --update
    ~/.local/bin/aws configure set region {REGION} --profile {PROFILE}
    ~/.local/bin/aws login --profile {PROFILE}          # browser sign-in; on a headless box add --remote

macOS: `brew install awscli`, then the same `aws configure set` and `aws login` lines.
If `aws sts get-caller-identity --profile {PROFILE}` already works, skip this section.

## 2. Run the bootstrap

    ~/.local/bin/aws s3 cp {SETUP_S3}/bootstrap.sh /tmp/bootstrap.sh --profile {PROFILE}
    AWS_PROFILE={PROFILE} bash /tmp/bootstrap.sh {SETUP_S3}

It is idempotent: re-run it any time, on any machine, and it converges to the same state. It backs up anything it
replaces under ~/.keys-backup/<stamp>/. What it does, step by step, is in the header of the script.

## 3. Read the ontology, then check that everything listed is installed

    . ~/.config/tracedb/env
    cat ~/ontology/AGENT.md                  # the protocol: ontolagni says where, traceDB fetches
    ontolagni find skills user installed --kind skill --limit 80   # every saved skill (user set first); table in ~/ontology/ONTOLOGY.md
    ontolagni find boxes                     # every box: status, hardware, shell, key, route, notes
    keys status                              # canonical keys/creds/Claude config vs this machine: all "in sync"
    skills status                            # user skill set installed identically
    keys doctor                              # every ssh alias: key present? reachable? accepted key?

What the ontology lists and how each kind is installed:

| what | source of truth | installed by |
|---|---|---|
| SSH keys, ~/.ssh/config, known_hosts | vault `keys/MANIFEST.json` + `keys/files/.ssh/*` | `keys pull` |
| service credentials (AWS, Modal, HF, Vast, gcloud, netrc, OpenAI/OpenRouter, ...) | vault `keys/files/...` | `keys pull` |
| ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, settings.json, memory (re-keyed to this machine's home) | vault `keys/files/.claude/...` | `keys pull` |
| skills (every variant from every repo) | main `skills/MANIFEST.json` + `skills/<name>/<variant>/` | `skills pull` (user set) |
| boxes (hardware, status, notes) | main `config/boxes.json` | read only; `keys boxes probe --publish` refreshes |
| ontology | `{BUCKET}/ontology/` | bootstrap step 11 |

After the bootstrap, start a NEW Claude Code session so the restored skills, CLAUDE.md and memory load.

## 4. Keeping every machine identical

- This machine has something newer (a key, a credential, a memory note, a skill): `keys push` / `skills push`
  (`keys add <file>` tracks a new credential file; `skills promote <name>` makes a skill user-level).
  Then other machines get it with `keys pull` / `skills pull` (or just re-run the bootstrap).
- One SSH key everywhere: `~/.ssh/id_ed25519_main` is offered first to every host. A new box gets its `.pub` in
  authorized_keys when it is created. `keys doctor` lists boxes that do not accept it yet.
- New or changed box: edit ~/.ssh/config, check `ssh -G <alias>`, `keys push`, `keys boxes probe --publish <alias>`,
  then `bash ~/code/traceDB/tools/rebuild_ontology.sh` (merges, never clobbers other writers' nodes).
- Other vault prefixes (`home/ssh/`, `home/...`) are snapshots written by machine auto-syncs. `keys pull` never reads them;
  `keys status` tells you when a snapshot has a key or a Host the canonical copy lacks.

## 5. Rules

- Never print key or token contents: names, sizes, fingerprints only.
- Never write to a remote box (authorized_keys included) without the user's explicit go-ahead. `keys doctor` and
  `keys boxes probe` are read-only.
- Never put secrets, IPs or hostnames in the public GitHub repos (traceDB, ontolagni). They live in the private stores.

Stores: main `{TRACEDB_STORE}` (redacted), vault `{VAULT_STORE}` (KMS-encrypted originals). AWS profile `{PROFILE}`.
